body:
Parent: #373
Phase: P1
Related UX programme: #386 and its child lanes
Depends on Core wall/opening semantics and coordinate/invalidation work; coordinate with #389

## User outcome
An architect can switch to 3D and trust that the view represents the same building objects as the plan, with predictable camera/navigation, selection and visibility, without implying unsupported free-form 3D authoring.

## Current gap
ARQ already has a real user-reachable 3D surface and shared plan/3D selection for bounded cases. Core 1.0 needs that surface hardened across all supported Release-1 building objects and project lifecycle states.

## In scope
- document the Core 3D representation for levels, straight walls and supported hosted openings/doors/windows;
- derive meshes only from canonical semantic state and accepted geometry algorithms;
- stable mapping from rendered pick targets to canonical semantic IDs;
- orthographic camera, orbit/pan/zoom/fit/reset behaviour and bounded saved/current view state;
- current-level/isolate/hide behaviour using one view-state authority;
- selection parity with plan/browser/inspector;
- explicit unsupported-edit handoff to plan/inspector rather than fake 3D handles;
- mesh/cache invalidation after supported semantic edits, undo/redo, level changes and reopen;
- loading/fallback/error states for WebGPU/WebGL support policy without misrepresenting renderer capability;
- benchmark first-open, interaction and model-update latency under #402;
- accessibility alternatives for controls and non-pointer navigation where applicable.

## Out of scope
Photorealistic rendering, free-form modelling, direct 3D semantic manipulation not already backed by typed operations, materials library or P5 visualisation features.

## Safety invariants
- mesh transforms are never canonical model mutations;
- stale mesh/object IDs cannot accept selection or Agent references;
- switching renderer backend/fallback cannot change project semantics;
- unsupported object representation is explicit rather than silently omitted if it materially affects the Core building view.

## Evidence
Object/mesh mapping tests; selection round trip plan -> 3D -> browser/inspector; edit/undo/redo invalidation; stale mesh injection; browser capability tests under supported renderer fallback; benchmark-project performance; publish/reopen visual/semantic parity.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] All required Core building objects have a documented 3D representation or explicit bounded limitation.
- [ ] 3D picks always resolve to current canonical semantic IDs.
- [ ] Supported plan edits update 3D without manual refresh or stale geometry.
- [ ] Camera/visibility state is clearly separated from canonical building data.
- [ ] Unsupported direct 3D edits do not expose misleading affordances.
- [ ] Required renderer fallback/support states match #219/#358/#359/#360 policy.
- [ ] First-open and interaction performance meets #402 budgets.

## ZEUS CTO reconciliation
#369 must run this with #389 and UX-4 integration before promoting coordinated 3D as a complete Core capability.

Parent: #373
Phase: P1
Related UX programme: #377, especially UX-2/UX-3
Depends on canonical Core operation paths for the edited objects; coordinate with #389

## User outcome
The floor-plan view behaves like a dependable architectural instrument: correct semantic linework, stable selection/snapping, predictable visibility and line weights, responsive navigation, and no mismatch between what the user edits and what the canonical model contains.

## Current gap
ARQ has a real interactive PlanCanvas and renderer infrastructure, but Core 1.0 needs the plan surface verified as a complete production projection across all supported Release-1 object/annotation types rather than a wall-focused canvas plus library capabilities.

## In scope
- define the bounded Core plan representation for levels, straight walls, hosted openings/doors/windows, rooms, dimensions, notes and underlays;
- ensure plan primitives are derived from canonical semantic state or explicit view/document state;
- establish architectural draw order and stable line-weight hierarchy for the Core object set;
- integrate hide/show/isolate/view visibility only through one documented view-state authority;
- guarantee hit testing, selection bounds and snap candidates correspond to the visible/semantic objects they represent;
- define behaviour for off-level/background/reference content and current-level emphasis where Core requires it;
- make renderer/cache rebuilds disposable and semantically idempotent;
- ensure large underlays and Core benchmark object counts remain within performance budgets;
- cover empty/loading/recomputing/error states without replacing the canonical model with visual fallback state;
- ensure plan output used by sheet/PDF comes from compatible projection logic so editor and document linework do not diverge silently.

## Out of scope
No general 2D illustration package, arbitrary CAD entity authoring, curved walls or advanced documentation beyond Core scope. Detailed interaction polish lives in #377 child issues and should extend this capability rather than fork its renderer/model contract.

## Safety invariants
- hidden renderer objects do not imply deleted model objects;
- selection/hit testing never binds to stale or wrong semantic IDs;
- a failed derived render does not mutate canonical project state;
- visual simplification at low zoom cannot change snap/operation semantics silently;
- sheet/PDF projection differences, if any, are explicit and tested.

## Evidence
Renderer/unit tests; object-by-object visual fixtures; selection/snap parity checks; stale-cache injection; browser protected-plan workflow; benchmark-project pan/zoom/select/authoring performance; plan-to-sheet linework comparison.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Every P1 semantic/annotation type has a documented Core plan representation.
- [ ] Visible selectable objects resolve to the correct canonical IDs.
- [ ] Snap/hit-test sources are consistent with displayed geometry within the accepted tolerance policy.
- [ ] Renderer/cache rebuild cannot change semantic state or identity.
- [ ] Plan and sheet projections cannot silently disagree for supported Core content.
- [ ] Benchmark navigation/selection/preview performance meets #402 budgets.
- [ ] UI/UX issues under #377 consume this plan authority rather than inventing parallel visual/model state.

## ZEUS CTO reconciliation
#369 should evaluate this together with #389 and the UX-3 lanes before calling the plan-authoring capability current.

Parent programme: #367
Implements the recurring mechanics defined by #369
Depends on: #317, #319, #368, #370; may consume #325 repository graph when current and authoritative for impact only

## Outcome
ZEUS can determine when a programme reconciliation is due, gather the correct merged lanes and current evidence, and produce a bounded CTO reconciliation work packet plus the next safe parallel work frontier without relying on a human to remember which agents finished what.

## Important authority boundary
This automation coordinates and prepares work. It does not get autonomous authority to merge, waive release gates, decide product scope, resolve ADRs or silently edit the repository. #369 remains the governed reconciliation role and stronger repository/product authorities remain unchanged.

## In scope
Build one deterministic reconciliation/checkpoint mechanism that can:

- identify the actual integration authority dynamically rather than assuming a branch name;
- record the last successful CTO reconciliation checkpoint by integration commit and timestamp;
- collect programme issues/PRs merged since that checkpoint using #367/#377 programme metadata;
- detect whether the 3-to-5-merge threshold, phase-gate threshold, high-risk contract change or staleness threshold has been reached;
- detect changed high-risk surfaces: persistence/file format, canonical model/operations, renderer/invalidation, command/selection, Agent proposal/permissions, connectors, imports/exports, security/privacy;
- combine issue dependencies, active claims, PR diffs and repository graph impact where available;
- identify shared-file/semantic-contract collisions across nominally different issue numbers;
- generate a reconciliation packet containing exact head, included lanes, likely integration risks, required evidence commands, unresolved review threads, capability states affected and human decisions still blocking promotion;
- produce a `next safe work frontier` separated into:
  - safe parallel mutation lanes;
  - serialised mutation lanes;
  - research/review-only lanes;
  - blocked/human-evidence lanes;
- update or comment on #369/#367 with the checkpoint output after a governed reconciliation completes;
- keep history so a later run can explain what changed since the previous reconciliation.

## Trigger design
Support at minimum:

1. manual `zeus reconcile` or equivalent;
2. phase-gate preflight;
3. automatic advisory trigger after the configured merged-lane threshold;
4. automatic advisory trigger after a protected/high-risk contract merge;
5. staleness advisory when active development continues without a checkpoint for the configured interval.

The highest-frequency trigger should be advisory. Do not launch overlapping mutation agents automatically just because the threshold is met.

## Failure modes to test
- merge happened but issue metadata is stale;
- PR touches files outside its declared ownership;
- two merged PRs changed the same interface from different issue trees;
- programme issue was closed without merged evidence;
- branch/default authority changed;
- repository graph cache is stale/truncated;
- required evidence command no longer exists;
- active claim remains after merge;
- one lane was reverted after merge;
- human-only evidence still blocks a phase despite all code issues being closed.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] Reconciliation due/not-due result is deterministic from repository state.
- [ ] Current integration head and merge set are discovered dynamically.
- [ ] Product #367 and UI/UX #377 lanes are reconciled into one collision/dependency view.
- [ ] Stale graph/metadata cannot be used as hard proof.
- [ ] Output names exact evidence required for the affected surfaces.
- [ ] Next-safe-work frontier never includes blocked issues or conflicting mutation owners.
- [ ] Reverted/superseded work is not counted as current capability merely because its original issue is closed.
- [ ] Automation cannot waive human/legal/device/release gates.
- [ ] #369 can consume the packet and post a reproducible checkpoint record.

## Agent progress protocol
Post the trigger algorithm, repository inputs, sample generated packet, false-positive/false-negative cases found and proof that an intentionally conflicting pair of lanes is serialised rather than recommended as safe parallel work.

Parent: #373
Phase: P1 architecture foundation
Depends on: #88 and #464 where tolerance/numeric choices affect reference identity or remapping
Related: #385, #417, #422, #430

## User outcome
ARQ can refer to meaningful parts of building elements, such as a wall endpoint, wall face, opening edge or level plane, in a way that survives ordinary valid edits when it should and becomes explicitly broken when it cannot. Dimensions, review comments, Agent proposals and future connectors must not guess by nearest geometry or display name.

## Problem
A stable element ID is not enough for associative documentation and cross-system work. Many features need a durable reference to a semantic subpart of an object. Ad hoc coordinate snapshots or nearest-geometry lookup will eventually produce silent retargeting after wall edits, splits, host changes or imports.

## In scope
- define a versioned typed `SemanticReference` / subreference contract;
- identify the bounded Core reference families needed for Release 1, such as element, wall start/end, supported wall face/centreline reference, opening edge/centre and level/reference plane where justified by current model semantics;
- define what reference identity means independently from renderer mesh indices and transient topology;
- define explicit behaviour for update, move, split, merge, delete, rehost and migration;
- define deterministic remap rules only where semantic continuity is provable;
- otherwise preserve a broken/stale reference with reason instead of silently choosing a replacement;
- serialize references in `.arq` with versioning and validation;
- include reference impact in typed operations so downstream dimensions/review/Agent work can invalidate or remap predictably;
- expose query/diagnostic APIs sufficient for inspector, dimensions and future Agent/connectors;
- define import/external-host boundary: foreign references carry source identity/provenance and cannot masquerade as native ARQ subreferences without an explicit mapping.

## Out of scope
No generic persistent topology naming solution for every future curved/parametric object. No nearest-geometry heuristic as authoritative recovery. No renderer vertex/triangle IDs in canonical references.

## Safety invariants
- rename/display-label changes never change semantic identity;
- renderer rebuilds never change semantic identity;
- deleted/non-continuous targets do not silently retarget;
- split/merge remapping must be operation-aware and tested;
- unresolved references remain inspectable and repairable.

## Evidence
Property tests for serialization/versioning; operation tests for wall move/split/delete; migration fixtures; dimension reference regressions; stale review/Agent proposal tests; plan/3D renderer rebuild test proving mesh identities do not matter.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] A typed/versioned Core semantic-reference contract exists.
- [ ] Core dimension targets can use it without coordinates/display names as authority.
- [ ] Supported edits preserve references only under explicit deterministic rules.
- [ ] Unsupported continuity produces an explicit broken/stale reference.
- [ ] References survive native publish/reopen and schema migration.
- [ ] Review/Agent/external-host systems can reuse the contract rather than invent local anchors.
- [ ] Renderer/topology cache rebuild cannot change canonical reference identity.

## ZEUS CTO reconciliation
#369 must include this contract in any cross-lane review involving dimensions, wall semantics, review anchors, Agent proposals or external connectors. A local reference system in one of those features is programme drift.

Parent programme: #367
Related: #370 capability ledger, #369 reconciler, #448 incident readiness.

## Outcome
ARQ has one release mechanism that distinguishes engineering/pre-release, internal, architect alpha, beta and stable/GA; knows which defects stop each stage; and never releases based on evidence from a different commit.

## In scope
- codify release stages and required evidence bundles;
- P0/P1/P2/P3/P4 bug severity definitions with examples relevant to CAD/BIM/data loss;
- release-stop rules for data loss, semantic corruption, wrong-document writes, security/privacy breach, false durability and unrecoverable migration;
- exact-head evidence manifest: commit, build artifacts, test/benchmark/eval results, browser/device evidence, schema/format versions, known issues, rollback target;
- ensure evidence from base branch or prior head cannot satisfy the final release gate silently;
- define waived/known-issue procedure requiring owner and documented user impact;
- rollback compatibility check for project schema/migrations;
- staged rollout/kill-switch requirements for Agent/connectors/cloud features as those phases activate;
- release notes generated from verified capability changes rather than every internal refactor.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Each release stage has explicit entry/exit requirements.
- [ ] Bug severity has ARQ-specific objective examples and release consequences.
- [ ] Release bundle fingerprints the exact code/build under release.
- [ ] A new commit after evidence invalidates or requires refreshed affected evidence.
- [ ] Known release-stop classes cannot be waived by ordinary agent action.
- [ ] Rollback includes data-format compatibility, not only code deployment.
- [ ] Capability/public claim promotion consumes #370 evidence state.

Parent programme: #367
Cross-cutting architecture issue.

## Outcome
ARQ can support different locales/languages later without making unit display, number parsing, decimal separators, paper sizes, dates or translated labels part of canonical geometric identity.

## In scope
- define locale-sensitive formatting boundary for numbers, dates, paper names and user-facing strings;
- keep canonical units/numeric representation governed by #88 independently from locale;
- metric/imperial input parsing rules with locale-aware decimal/group separators where supported;
- stable command/object/type IDs separate from translated display names;
- translatable UI copy architecture and generated command/help references;
- right-to-left readiness review at layout/token level without promising immediate RTL support;
- font/glyph fallback policy for notes/UI vs PDF export;
- project-level preferred display units/format separate from user UI language;
- export/import formatting rules that must remain machine-stable regardless of locale.

## Safety
Never parse geometry using translated labels. Never change stored geometry because UI locale changed. Never silently reinterpret `1,5`/`1.5` without a documented input policy.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Locale and canonical numeric/unit responsibilities are explicitly separated.
- [ ] Stable IDs/commands are not translated keys.
- [ ] Number input/output has deterministic tests for supported locale patterns.
- [ ] PDF/document font strategy can represent supported note text without silent character loss.
- [ ] Future translation can be added without rewriting semantic schemas.
- [ ] Unsupported locale/input forms produce clear validation rather than wrong geometry.

Parent programme: #367
Related: #218, #216 and later collaboration/account phases.

## Outcome
ARQ has one understandable lifecycle for local projects, cloud/team metadata, review data and user account deletion, without conflating deleting a cloud record with destroying a user's portable `.arq` file.

## In scope
- define archive vs delete vs remove-from-recent vs remove-local-working-copy;
- define what is soft-deleted, hard-deleted, recoverable and for how long only after owner/legal/ops decisions exist;
- local `.arq` files remain user-owned external files unless the user explicitly deletes them outside ARQ or a native file action clearly targets them;
- working-copy cleanup rules and last-known-good/recovery data lifecycle;
- review/share link revocation and collaborator access removal;
- account deletion impact on projects the user owns vs projects shared with them;
- export/data portability before destructive cloud/account operations;
- audit/event history retention boundaries;
- future firm/Agent/connector data included explicitly as phases activate.

## UX
Destructive actions must name the object and consequence precisely. 'Delete project' cannot mean different things in local vs cloud contexts. Irreversible actions require confirmation appropriate to risk; reversible archive/remove-from-recent actions should not be over-confirmed.

## Safety
No background cleanup may delete the user's source `.arq` file. Retention durations/regions/vendors are human/business/legal decisions, not constants agents may invent.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Lifecycle matrix covers local file, OPFS working copy, recovery data, cloud metadata, review data and account state.
- [ ] UI terminology maps one-to-one to actual deletion consequences.
- [ ] Restore/revoke paths are tested where supported.
- [ ] Data portability/export exists before irreversible cloud/account deletion in applicable phases.
- [ ] Retention implementation consumes approved #218/#216 decisions rather than guessing them.

Parent programme: #367
Related human/business decisions: #218

## Outcome
When ARQ fails, users and support can collect enough technical evidence to diagnose the problem without uploading private architectural content by default, and the team has a real incident process before stable release.

## In scope
### Local diagnostics/support bundle
- app/version/build/revision;
- browser/OS/device/capability summary;
- `.arq` schema/application version and non-content integrity state;
- Worker/session lifecycle diagnostics;
- recent command/operation **types and IDs only where privacy policy permits**, not raw private geometry/content by default;
- renderer/backend capability;
- performance timings;
- import/export adapter/version/result metadata;
- error codes/stacks with secret/path/content redaction;
- Agent/connector tool/proposal IDs and versions without prompts/project text unless explicitly consented;
- user-controlled preview before export.

### Product observability
Define anonymous/consented telemetry boundaries separately from support bundles. No hidden upload of project geometry, filenames, client names, note text or imported properties.

### Incident readiness
- severity model aligned with programme P0/P1 bug definitions;
- owner/on-call/contact process appropriate to company stage;
- data-loss/security incident playbooks;
- rollback/build-disable/connector-disable mechanisms;
- known-issue communication and recovery guidance;
- post-incident evidence/update procedure.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Support bundle is user-reachable, inspectable before sharing and privacy-safe by default.
- [ ] Secret/private-content redaction has automated tests.
- [ ] Diagnostics can distinguish project-format, Worker/storage, renderer, import, Agent and connector failures.
- [ ] Telemetry is opt-in/contracted according to approved privacy policy and not required for local authoring.
- [ ] P0 data-loss/security incident runbook and rollback path exist before broad beta/stable release.
- [ ] #218 decisions are reflected without inventing retention/vendor/region values prematurely.

Parent programme: #367
Cross-cutting security issue. Applies from P1 through P10.

## Outcome
Opening or importing a hostile/corrupt `.arq`, image, PDF, DXF, IFC or later external file must not execute code, escape the intended parser boundary, exhaust unbounded resources, corrupt an already-open project, or create misleading partial-success state.

## In scope
- inventory every untrusted file/parser entry point and the process/Worker boundary it uses;
- byte/size/count/depth/time limits before and during parse;
- cancellation and timeout semantics;
- decompression/archive-bomb protections where applicable;
- SQLite/application-id/schema/integrity handling for `.arq`;
- PDF/image decoder safety and resource bounding;
- DXF/IFC pathological entity/count/string/nesting fixtures;
- sanitise external strings used in UI, logs, Agent context and generated documents;
- no dynamic code execution from imported content;
- keep staging/import transaction isolated from canonical state until commit;
- security regression corpus/fuzz inputs with licences/provenance recorded;
- support-safe error diagnostics that do not leak file contents.

## Safety / UX
Rejected files get format-specific actionable errors when known. Unknown/unsafe cases fail closed. A failed import never leaves half-created canonical objects. Existing open project remains usable.

## Acceptance
- [ ] All current file boundaries are enumerated with owner/parser/runtime limits.
- [ ] Fuzz/adversarial fixtures cover current native/import formats.
- [ ] Resource exhaustion is bounded/cancellable.
- [ ] External text cannot inject executable markup/tool instructions into privileged surfaces.
- [ ] Import/open failures are atomic relative to canonical project state.
- [ ] Security checks are selected automatically when parser/file-boundary code changes.
- [ ] Future adapters must satisfy this contract before release.

Parent: #372
Phase: P0
Related: #367, #368, #370

## Outcome
The universal trajectory should live inside the repository as a versioned planning/control document so agents do not depend on private chat context. It must be advisory below stronger implementation/ADR evidence, but stable enough to trace issues and phase gates.

## In scope
- add the approved ARQ Universal Product Trajectory and Master Programme Plan under a clear `docs/product/` or `docs/programme/` path;
- preserve its authority/evidence rules and snapshot-freshness warning;
- add stable requirement namespaces/IDs for phase gates, product journeys, cross-cutting invariants and no-go conditions;
- link #367 and phase epics to the relevant requirement IDs;
- add a changelog/version header and supersession procedure;
- define what parts are generated from #370 versus manually governed strategy text;
- add a freshness check so dated repository facts cannot masquerade as current execution truth;
- avoid duplicating accepted ADR content: link to it instead.

## Out of scope
Rewriting current ADRs, auto-changing product scope from market research, or treating the trajectory document as proof that a feature exists.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Universal trajectory is committed and discoverable from README/START-HERE or equivalent contributor entrypoint.
- [ ] Requirement IDs are stable and referenced by programme issues.
- [ ] Current-state tables either generate from #370 or carry explicit snapshot dates/freshness state.
- [ ] Contradictions with stronger sources fail a validation/check or are explicitly surfaced.
- [ ] A documented update procedure requires evidence/decision references for material roadmap changes.
- [ ] Historical versions remain traceable without competing as current authority.

Parent programme: #367
Entry: earlier phases prove ARQ can preserve semantic/project trust while extending capability.

## Product outcome
ARQ grows from the focused architectural product into an extensible professional platform without turning the core into an ungoverned plugin host or attempting every incumbent feature at once.

## Candidate capability families
Revalidate and prioritise against real users before decomposing:
- curved walls and richer profile/geometry types;
- advanced stairs/railings and roofs;
- reusable parametric components/families;
- sections/elevations beyond the bounded earlier documentation scope;
- schedules and quantity/document systems;
- renovation phases/design options maturity;
- BCF/consultant coordination and additional exchange formats;
- selected structural/MEP/reference-analysis integrations, not necessarily native authoring;
- desktop shells where platform evidence justifies them;
- public extension/plugin SDK;
- signed connector/Skill/component distribution and marketplace governance.

## Extension principles
- extensions operate through versioned capability APIs, typed operations and permission scopes;
- no direct arbitrary mutation of canonical database internals;
- compatibility and migration rules are explicit;
- extension failure cannot corrupt the project or block safe-mode open;
- packages/Skills/connectors are signed/identified under an approved distribution/security model;
- marketplace/business incentives never override data portability or project access.

## Exit gate for a mature ecosystem
- [ ] Extension SDK has stable versioning, capability discovery and sandbox/permission model.
- [ ] Project safe-open can disable incompatible/broken extensions.
- [ ] At least one third-party-style extension path is proven end to end without privileged core access.
- [ ] Advanced architectural capabilities are decomposed and shipped through the same semantic/operation/document rules as Core.
- [ ] Security review covers supply-chain, signing, malicious extensions and secret access.
- [ ] Marketplace/moderation/licensing/support policies exist before public distribution.

## Decomposition rule
Do not pre-create hundreds of speculative feature issues. When P10 approaches, #369 must combine current user research, market state, capability ledger and architecture constraints to create bounded epics/issues for the highest-value professional gaps. #367 closes only when this phase is either completed or deliberately superseded by an owner-approved next universal trajectory.

Parent programme: #367
Entry: review/revision identity proven in P2; local canonical operations/persistence mature.

## Product outcome
Teams can review and eventually edit the same architectural project without turning collaboration into a second source of truth or allowing silent conflict resolution to damage semantic relationships.

## Capability sequence
1. revision-bound comments/issues and review state from P2;
2. authenticated team project metadata, roles and explicit sync state;
3. operation/snapshot sync with offline queues and conflict visibility;
4. design options/branches and semantic comparison;
5. only then, concurrent semantic editing for carefully selected operation classes.

## Non-negotiable rules
- local project state and cloud sync state are separate concepts in UI;
- server cannot make an unacknowledged local edit disappear silently;
- every remote operation names project/revision/actor and deterministic preconditions;
- conflicts on topology/host/dependency-sensitive edits must not be auto-merged by naive last-write-wins;
- presence/cursors are ephemeral derived collaboration state, not canonical model content;
- permission changes/revocation apply at server and client boundaries;
- design options have explicit ancestry and merge/reconcile semantics.

## Exit gate
- [ ] Sync/offline/reconnect data-loss matrix passes.
- [ ] Roles/permissions are enforced server-side and surfaced honestly.
- [ ] Revision/operation provenance answers who changed what and from which base.
- [ ] Design option comparison/merge cannot silently discard canonical content.
- [ ] Concurrent editing ships only for operation classes with a proven conflict model.
- [ ] Collaboration outage never prevents access to an already available local project unless the explicit product policy says otherwise.
- [ ] #369 reconciliation validates local/native authority remains coherent.

## Decomposition rule
When P8 activates, create separate atomic issues for identity/RBAC, sync transport, offline queue, conflict UI, revision history, design options, and each concurrent-operation family. Never implement 'real-time collaboration' as one broad issue.

Parent programme: #367
Entry: dependable Core model/revision/view architecture; P4 may proceed independently where dependencies permit, but do not let visual work replace Core completeness.

## Product outcome
An architect can move from a coordinated ARQ view to client-ready architectural visualisation and presentation while preserving a clear boundary between true project geometry and AI/creative imagery.

## Required capability layers

### Strict-geometry visualisation
- render/generation starts from an explicit ARQ project revision + saved camera/view;
- building geometry is locked to the supported semantic model/projection;
- materials/environment/lighting/landscape/occupancy may vary only inside the declared contract;
- output records revision, camera, settings/model provenance;
- geometry drift detection/evaluation is required before calling a mode geometry-preserving.

### Creative mode
- clearly labelled as concept/creative output;
- may alter/add content according to explicit controls;
- never silently feeds visual invention back into canonical BIM geometry;
- any reverse-to-model workflow must become a separate reviewed proposal/conversion.

### Present
- saved cameras/scenes;
- ordered presentation/story views;
- bounded walkthrough/path generation;
- revision-linked output with stale indicators after project changes;
- client-facing presentation surface without accidental authoring.

## Lessons to apply
Study current Velocity, D5, Veras, LookX and relevant tools when this phase begins, but revalidate their actual current capabilities before implementation.

## Exit gate
- [ ] Strict mode has measured geometry-fidelity tests.
- [ ] Every generated output names source project revision/view/camera.
- [ ] Creative and geometry-preserving modes cannot be confused in UI or provenance.
- [ ] Project changes can mark prior renders/presentations stale.
- [ ] Presentation/walkthrough output is reproducible enough for support and does not become canonical model state.
- [ ] Privacy/cost/provider failure states are productised.

## Decomposition rule
When P5 becomes active, ZEUS/#369 creates bounded issues for provider/renderer architecture, geometry-lock evals, saved cameras, strict mode, creative mode and presentation/walkthrough. Do not implement this epic as one lane.

Parent programme: #367
Entry: P3 native Agent gate green. **Blocked from implementation until then except research/spikes.**

## Product outcome
ARQ Agent can understand and safely operate supported external design applications through host-specific typed adapters, while the host file remains authoritative for host-native content and ARQ never pretends generic screen automation is semantic CAD control.

Target host family for research: Revit, SketchUp, AutoCAD/Civil 3D where relevant, Rhino/Grasshopper, Blender, Archicad. Connector order is not pre-decided.

## Architecture contract
Two layers:

1. **Host-side adapter/plugin**: current document identity, revision/change token, selection/view context, capability discovery, typed read/write tools, transaction/undo integration, event stream, screenshots only as supplemental context, result verification.
2. **Shared ARQ Agent**: planning, proposals, permission checks, validation, review, provenance, Skills and orchestration.

## Required Host Adapter SDK
The future atomic issue set must cover:
- handshake/version/capability discovery;
- host/document/project stable identity;
- current selection/view/context snapshot;
- typed query tools;
- typed proposal/execute tools;
- permission levels from read-only through bounded write;
- host-native transaction and grouped undo;
- stale-document/precondition detection;
- operation result + post-action verification;
- event/change notifications;
- cancellation/timeouts/crash recovery;
- secret/auth transport and local-network boundaries;
- provenance linking ARQ proposal to host transaction IDs;
- test double/reference host for SDK conformance.

## Connector 0 bake-off
Before selecting the first production connector, build bounded read/query + one safe reversible mutation prototypes for **Revit and SketchUp** (or replace a candidate only with recorded current evidence). Score:
- API semantic richness;
- identity/change tracking;
- transaction/undo quality;
- deployment/install friction;
- cross-platform reach;
- architecture market value;
- test automation feasibility;
- failure isolation;
- licensing/distribution constraints;
- support burden;
- Agent capability coverage.

Do not choose based on the easiest demo.

## Safety invariants
- no blind clicking as the canonical write path;
- every write names exact host/document identity and fresh preconditions;
- changing active document invalidates pending proposals;
- connector cannot silently escalate permission;
- host adapter verifies post-action result before reporting success;
- ARQ native project and host project authorities remain distinct;
- no arbitrary unreviewed host scripting in early production connectors.

## Phase exit gate
- [ ] Host Adapter SDK contract + conformance harness stable.
- [ ] Connector 0 selected from measured bake-off, not assumption.
- [ ] First connector supports useful read context and at least one reviewed reversible write end to end.
- [ ] Wrong-document/stale-document/permission/crash tests fail closed.
- [ ] Host-native undo and post-action verification proven.
