Parent: #419
Phase: P3
Depends on: #421, #422, #424; Skills tests extend it through #426.

## Outcome
ARQ has repeatable evidence that Agent behaviour stays bounded, source-grounded and non-destructive as models/tools/prompts change.

## Eval categories
- read-only factual project queries with known answers;
- unsupported/unknown fact requests;
- ambiguous architectural requests that require clarification;
- stale project/revision context;
- deleted/moved target object;
- dangerous/destructive requests;
- invalid geometry and validation refusal;
- permission/grant expiry/revocation;
- cross-project context isolation;
- proposal diff correctness;
- exact reviewed proposal vs applied operation equality;
- grouped undo/reopen persistence;
- prompt/tool injection through project names, notes, imported properties and external files;
- privacy/redaction boundaries;
- repeated-run nondeterminism where only proposal prose may vary but operation constraints must hold;
- Skill permission/escalation attempts.

## Scoring
Separate model-quality scores from hard safety invariants. Hard invariants are pass/fail and cannot be averaged away by good conversational quality.

## Release policy
Provider/model/prompt/tool-contract changes that affect Agent behaviour must run the relevant eval set before release. Baselines are versioned by exact model/tool/contract configuration.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Committed deterministic fixtures cover all hard invariant categories.
- [ ] Safety failures are release-blocking at the appropriate Agent phase.
- [ ] Eval output records exact model/tool/skill/config versions.
- [ ] Cross-project/stale-revision tests fail closed.
- [ ] Prompt/tool injection fixtures cannot gain hidden authority.
- [ ] Applied operations are mechanically compared with reviewed proposals.
- [ ] The suite can be extended for P4 external-host connectors without conflating native and host permissions.

Parent: #419
Phase: P3
Depends on: #422/#424

## User outcome
Architects/firms can reuse a trusted multi-step ARQ workflow without converting it into an opaque macro or giving it more authority than its declared capabilities.

## Skill contract
A Skill must declare:
- stable ID/name/version;
- purpose and human-readable description;
- required ARQ/host capabilities;
- input parameter schema and defaults;
- ordered tool/analysis/proposal steps;
- preconditions and revision assumptions;
- review/approval gates;
- deterministic validators;
- expected outputs/artifacts;
- permissions required;
- rollback/undo expectations;
- compatibility range;
- provenance/version/hash;
- test/eval fixtures.

## In scope
Skill registry/loading, version selection, compatibility checking, execution plan preview, parameter UI, bounded execution through existing Agent proposal/mutation contracts, result/provenance record, import/export of skill definitions if security policy permits.

## Safety
Skills cannot embed secrets, bypass tool grants, call arbitrary unsandboxed code by default, or silently expand requested permissions. Updating a Skill version does not rewrite the provenance of previous runs.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Skills are versioned typed data, not prompt snippets.
- [ ] Required capabilities/permissions are checked before run.
- [ ] Skill execution uses ordinary proposals/approval where changes are consequential.
- [ ] Incompatible/stale skills fail clearly before mutation.
- [ ] Previous run provenance identifies the exact Skill version/hash.
- [ ] Eval fixtures prove deterministic validators/review gates cannot be skipped.
- [ ] At least two bounded useful Core/Documentation Skills demonstrate the contract.

Parent: #419
Phase: P3
