## User outcome
When ARQ Agent suggests a change, the architect sees exactly what would change, why, which objects/documents are affected, what assumptions were made, and whether deterministic validation passes before anything is committed.

## In scope
Define one proposal object with at least:
- proposal ID;
- project/document/revision precondition;
- user request and bounded interpreted intent;
- assumptions/questions;
- typed operation group, not hidden geometry edits;
- affected semantic IDs and derived/document impacts;
- validation results/warnings/errors;
- permission/approval requirement;
- provenance: model/tool/skill/version/context;
- expiry/stale reason;
- dry-run/diff representation.

Build a Review Centre UI showing semantic before/after, operation list, warnings, assumptions, affected views/sheets and explicit Approve/Reject. Review UI must be keyboard accessible and not rely on colour alone.

## Safety
This issue does not apply proposals. A proposal generated against revision N becomes stale when its preconditions are no longer true. No automatic 'refresh and apply' that changes semantics without a new review.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Proposal schema is typed/versioned and independent of model-provider prose.
- [ ] Review Centre renders real proposals against current project context.
- [ ] Stale/revision-mismatched proposals are visibly blocked.
- [ ] Deterministic validation output is distinguished from AI rationale.
- [ ] Affected semantic/document surfaces are explicit.
- [ ] Approve cannot commit yet until the governed mutation issue lands.
- [ ] Tests cover stale proposal, deleted target, permission mismatch, invalid operation and multi-operation diff.

Parent: #410
Phase: P2

## User outcome
An architect can share a bounded review state, receive comments/issues tied to the correct project revision/object/view, compare revisions, and resolve feedback without allowing review metadata to mutate architectural geometry implicitly.

## In scope
- immutable/revision-bound review snapshot identity;
- share/viewer/commenter permissions within the current security model;
- comments and issues anchored to revision + view + semantic object/subreference where possible;
- stale-anchor handling when later revisions remove/change targets;
- issue state/assignee only if supported by current account/RBAC policy;
- semantic revision comparison for Core objects and document changes;
- visual highlighting as derived presentation of semantic diff;
- review data lifecycle/export/delete policy;
- offline/native authoring remains usable without review service availability.

## Safety
Review comments never become canonical building operations. A comment on revision N must not silently jump to a 'nearest' object in revision N+1. Private project content/access rules must be enforced server-side when sharing is introduced.

## UX
Reviewer can understand which revision/view they are seeing. Author can filter open/resolved issues and jump to valid anchors. Stale anchors show why they are stale. Comparison distinguishes added/removed/changed, and does not rely only on colour.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Review snapshots are revision-bound and immutable once shared.
- [ ] Comment/issue anchors include explicit revision/object context.
- [ ] Stale anchors never silently retarget.
- [ ] Revision comparison is based on semantic/document identity, not screenshot pixel diff alone.
- [ ] Review service failure does not block local authoring.
- [ ] Permission/access tests cover unauthorised links and revoked access.
- [ ] Accessibility and non-colour diff cues are proven.

Parent: #410
