## User outcome
Before committing imported external data, ARQ explains what it understood, how units/coordinates were resolved, what will become native/reference data, and what will be preserved, approximated, flattened, omitted or kept opaque.

## In scope
Define one adapter-neutral contract containing:
- source format/version and provenance;
- parser/adapter version;
- source units and coordinate system;
- proposed target transform;
- entity/object counts by disposition;
- `preserved`, `approximated`, `flattened`, `omitted`, `opaque_preserved`, `unsupported` outcomes;
- warnings/errors and severity;
- preview/staging identity;
- explicit commit/cancel transaction boundary;
- export equivalent report for lossy/bounded formats;
- report persistence/provenance policy.

## UX
One consistent review surface across DXF/IFC and later adapters. Critical loss must be impossible to miss. Large verbose diagnostics may be expandable, but the summary must state material loss plainly.

## Safety
Adapters must not bypass staging and mutate canonical state directly. The fidelity report cannot be fabricated from adapter capability docs alone; it must describe the actual file/job result.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Shared typed fidelity/staging contract exists.
- [ ] DXF and IFC paths can consume it without format-specific UI forks for common concepts.
- [ ] Import commit is atomic and cancel/failure leaves canonical state unchanged.
- [ ] Material omission/flattening requires visible acknowledgement where appropriate.
- [ ] Reports identify source/adapter/transform for later support/debugging.
- [ ] Tests prove an adapter cannot report success while silently dropping a fixture's known unsupported content.

