## Outcome
Put the protected Core workflow in front of a small controlled group of real architects, observe task completion and failures, and convert findings into bounded issues before expanding feature scope.

## Alpha entry gate
- protected Core release harness green on the intended build;
- native durability/recovery matrix green for required rows;
- supported browser/device envelope explicit;
- legal/privacy wording sufficient for controlled testing;
- support/incident contact and recovery procedure available;
- no P0 data-loss/security blocker.

## Required alpha tasks
Participants should attempt realistic tasks, not click through a scripted demo:
- create or open a project;
- trace or author a small residential plan;
- edit walls/openings;
- create/inspect rooms;
- dimension/note;
- inspect 3D;
- create a plan sheet and PDF;
- close/reopen;
- recover from at least one non-destructive simulated failure or understand recovery guidance.

Record task completion, failure point, workaround, severity, terminology confusion, performance problem, accessibility/input problem and whether the user would trust the project file afterward. Do not convert a small alpha into invented market statistics.

## Alpha issue discipline
Every P0/P1 finding gets a bounded bug issue with reproduction/evidence. Do not bury critical problems in a research summary. Nice-to-have feature requests go to the future-phase backlog only after checking scope.

## Exit gate
- [ ] At least the approved minimum number/mix of real architect sessions is completed.
- [ ] No unresolved alpha finding meets P0/release-stop severity.
- [ ] P1 critical workflow blockers are fixed or explicitly defer the alpha exit.
- [ ] Usability problems have severity and evidence, not just opinions.
- [ ] Recovery/file-trust feedback is reviewed specifically.
- [ ] #369 reconciliation pass reruns the protected workflow after alpha fixes.
- [ ] P2 may start only after the owner accepts the resulting Core baseline.

