## User outcome
ARQ should have one reproducible exact-head proof that the first promised architectural workflow works as one system, rather than a collection of passing package tests.

## Protected scenario
Using a committed benchmark fixture and/or deterministic scripted creation path:
1. create/open a supported project;
2. establish units/level;
3. optionally place/calibrate an underlay;
4. create and modify semantic walls;
5. place door/window openings;
6. create a valid room;
7. place dimensions and a text note;
8. inspect the same objects in 3D/browser/inspector;
9. create one plan sheet;
10. export scaled vector PDF;
11. publish native `.arq`;
12. close all project workers/sessions;
13. independently reopen;
14. verify semantic equivalence and the intended document state;
15. exercise one recovery/failure path without losing acknowledged work.

## Evidence rules
The harness must drive the real production bundle and real Worker/storage/export paths for product claims. It may seed deterministic fixture input, but it must not reimplement the mechanism under test.

Record exact revision, browser/engine, fixture hashes, timing, evidence outputs and failure reproduction details. A green harness proves only its defined support envelope.

## Acceptance
- [ ] One command runs the protected workflow against a production build.
- [ ] Failure at any stage makes the gate fail with actionable evidence.
- [ ] Native reopened state is semantically compared, not merely checked for file existence.
- [ ] Vector PDF scale/content is validated.
- [ ] Plan/3D/document coordination is asserted after edits.
- [ ] A recovery/failure scenario is included.
- [ ] Gate consumes current platform policy and does not overclaim physical-device coverage.
- [ ] P1 cannot be marked green while this harness is red or mandatory human/platform gates are unresolved.

