## User outcome
A keyboard user and a screen-reader user can operate the supported Core desktop workflow without focus loss, inaccessible controls, colour-only state or modal traps. Touch targets and responsive layouts remain usable on supported tablet surfaces.

## In scope
Audit the actual Core workflow, not only design-system primitives:
- tool rail and active-tool state;
- command palette and dialogs;
- project browser/tree;
- inspector forms;
- canvas focus and keyboard commands;
- numeric overlays;
- underlay controls;
- door/window/room/dimension/note tools;
- 3D tab/navigation controls;
- sheet/document controls;
- file/open/publish/export/recovery dialogs;
- status messages, errors, toasts and banners.

Require accessible names, visible focus, logical tab order, focus restoration, non-colour state cues, correct roles/states, 44px touch targets where the current contract requires them, reduced-motion handling where relevant, and no keyboard traps.

Canvas geometry itself does not need to become a DOM tree of every line, but essential selection/tool state and commands must have accessible alternatives/status communication.

## Evidence
Automated accessibility checks are necessary but not sufficient. Add deterministic keyboard/focus browser tests and manual screen-reader smoke evidence for the release envelope. Physical VoiceOver/iPad evidence stays within #359 where required.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Complete Core benchmark can be traversed with keyboard for all non-canvas-precision actions.
- [ ] All interactive controls have names, states and focus indicators.
- [ ] Dialogs/sheets restore focus and cannot trap/lose it incorrectly.
- [ ] Critical state is never conveyed by colour alone.
- [ ] Error/save/recovery status is announced appropriately.
- [ ] No known critical WCAG/input blocker remains for the supported Core workflow.

Parent: #373
