## User outcome
An architect can trust that acknowledged Core work survives normal close/reopen and that interrupted writes, crashes, migrations, storage errors and failed publication produce explicit recoverable states rather than silent loss.

## In scope
Build one end-to-end durability matrix covering at least:
- new project -> edit -> close -> reopen;
- existing flat project -> edit -> publish -> independent reopen;
- rich reference project -> semantic edit -> publish -> independent reopen;
- undo/redo before close;
- close while save queued/in flight;
- Worker crash/messageerror;
- browser/tab abnormal termination and restart recovery;
- migration success and migration failure/quarantine;
- integrity-check failure;
- WAL/sidecar refusal cases;
- read-only version floors;
- interrupted/failed publication;
- corrupted derived cache vs corrupted canonical database;
- source file unchanged until explicit publication contract says otherwise;
- multiple project switches without wrong-project responses or leaked writers.

## Product UX
Every matrix row needs an honest user-visible state and recovery action. Do not rely only on console diagnostics.

## Evidence
Use real bundled Worker, sqlite-wasm, OPFS and production app paths in browser tests for the rows that depend on those mechanisms. Unit mocks are supplemental only.

## Acceptance
- [ ] Matrix is machine-readable and mapped to capability/release gates.
- [ ] No acknowledged Core edit is lost in passing cases.
- [ ] Failure cases never report `saved`/`published` incorrectly.
- [ ] Recovery does not resurrect superseded edits or cross project identities.
- [ ] Failed migrations preserve evidence/last-known-good state per policy.
- [ ] Independent reopen verifies published outputs.
- [ ] All P0/P1 durability rows required for alpha are green or explicitly release-blocking.

