## User outcome

A user should never have multiple competing meanings of save/publish/export or multiple code paths that can produce different `.arq` results from the same working project.

## Current gap

The repository has native working-copy persistence, `publishNativeProject`, and a second publication implementation (`publishProjectFile`) that has been described as unwired. Parallel implementations are a future data-integrity and maintenance risk.

## In scope

- identify every current native save/checkpoint/publish/export path and every UI command that can reach them;
- choose one canonical publication authority based on current ADRs and implementation evidence;
- route all product publication through that authority;
- remove, deprecate or explicitly bound duplicate paths so they cannot drift;
- define exact vocabulary and state transitions for `unsaved`, `saving`, `saved to working copy`, `publishing`, `published`, `publish failed`, `read-only` and `recovery required`;
- ensure publication drains/coordinates pending native writes rather than racing them;
- always reopen exported bytes in an independent reader before reporting success;
- compare project identity, revision and semantic hash/equivalence as appropriate;
- preserve original selected files until explicit publication/write-back semantics exist;
- make failure non-destructive and retryable.

## Out of scope

- solving rich semantic editing itself, which is #356;
- inventing cloud sync;
- direct destructive overwrite of the user's source file without an explicit approved contract.

## UI/UX

- one canonical Publish command and status path;
- disabled states explain why publication is unavailable;
- success copy must describe exactly what was written and where;
- failure must keep the working project open and visibly unsaved/unpublished;
- closing with pending native writes or unpublished changes must follow an explicit tested policy.

## Tests / evidence

- flat project edit -> native save -> publish -> independent reopen;
- rich project after #356 -> publish -> independent reopen with untouched-content equivalence;
- pending save while publish begins;
- failed checkpoint;
- failed export;
- failed independent reopen/semantic comparison;
- read-only project;
- migration-required project;
- repeated publish idempotence where expected;
- browser-level capability check through the real command path.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Exactly one product publication authority remains reachable.
- [ ] Duplicate implementations cannot silently diverge.
- [ ] Published success requires independent reopen/verification.
- [ ] Save and publish states are distinct and accurate in the UI.
- [ ] Publish waits for or explicitly resolves in-flight native persistence.
- [ ] Failure never destroys the current working copy or lies about durability.
- [ ] Exact-head browser evidence proves the real product path.
- [ ] The capability ledger can identify one canonical native publication capability.

## Handoff

#369 must review this alongside #355/#356 because integration errors across these three lanes can produce the most serious early ARQ data-loss bugs.

